A solution is feasible if all hard constraints are satisfied by the solution. Accord- ing to the organisers of the ITC-2007, a timetable is required to satisfy all hard constraints. Due to the fact that it may be very difficult to achieve this hard con- straints feasibility, it was suggested that some events may be left unassigned in a timetable if necessary, and they introduced the notion of “distance to feasibility (Df)” [146], which is defined as the number of students that are affected by unas- signed events, was introduced. Given a solution, if there is any event that causes any hard-constraint violation, it needs to be removed (i.e., unassigned) from the timetable, and as a consequence, some students who have to take this event will suffer from its removal. The Df of the given solution is calculated by identifying the number of students that are required to attend each of the unassigned events and then simply adding these values together. For example, if a solution has three events that need to be unassigned to prevent any violation of the hard constraints,
